<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · other</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gonghe Regency</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">841–828 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gonghe Regency was the period from 841 to 828 BCE when the Zhou state was governed without a king after a revolt in the capital drove King Li into exile. Traditional sources describe joint rule by the Dukes of Zhou and Shao, while others credit a noble named He, the Earl of Gong. The year 841 BCE is the first securely dated year in Chinese history, recorded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Records of the Grand Historian</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gonghe-regency.jpg" alt="Gonghe Regency" width="320" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Xuan_(monarch_of_the_Zhou_dynasty).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
